big: 1 and 2 Chronicles
lede: The same centuries, told again by someone with a temple to justify and a congregation to hold together. What it leaves out is famous. What it adds is more interesting.
calls:
k: Why this is a focused study
Chronicles isn't a new story. It's a second telling. Nine pages are enough to hold what changed, why a community after the exile would want it changed, and what an empire learns from a sanctioned retelling.
||
k: Read it against Samuel–Kings
This spread only works in comparison. Keep a Samuel–Kings text open beside it, or the differences stay invisible.
/end

@page rh="CHRONICLES · 1 OF 6 · THE BOOK ON THE RECORD" m="1 CHR 1–9; 2 CHR 36.22–23; JAPHET, I & II CHRONICLES; WILLIAMSON, ISRAEL IN CHRONICLES; 1–2 KGS (COMPARE)"
# What it is
rec:
Name | The Hebrew title, *Divrei Hayyamim*, means "the events of the days," an annals title. The Greek tradition called it *Paraleipomena*, "the things left out," which is a judgment about the book wearing the clothes of a label.
Where it sits | In the Jewish ordering it's the last book of the whole Bible, and it ends with a Persian king telling the exiles to go up. In the Christian ordering it sits after Kings, in the middle. Same text, two different final words.
When and by whom | Anonymous, and generally placed in the Persian period, after the return. Whether the same hand wrote Ezra–Nehemiah was long assumed and is now widely questioned. Treat "the Chronicler" as a label for an unknown writer and not as an identified person.
Structure | Nine chapters of genealogy. David, at length. Solomon and the temple. The kings of Judah only, down to the exile, closing with the decree of Cyrus. The northern kingdom mostly isn't its subject.
Its sources | It draws on Samuel and Kings, often nearly word for word, and also cites records the reader can't check. It's a book openly built out of other books, which makes it the Bible's clearest case of a tradition editing itself.
Its purpose | Theological, liturgical and genealogical, written for a community after the exile that needed to know it was continuous with what came before. INTERPRETATION: Calling it "the authorized version" or "state-sponsored scripture" is an argument about that purpose. Use those phrases as arguments you're making and never as facts you're reporting.
box: What the book is actually doing
INTERPRETATION: Proving that a small, occupied province with a rebuilt temple is the same people as the kingdom in the old stories. Continuity is the argument, the genealogies are the evidence and the temple is the proof.
/end
prompt: What I assumed Chronicles was, before reading it next to Kings | fill

@page rh="CHRONICLES · 2 OF 6 · WHAT CHANGED IN THE RETELLING" m="2 SAM 11–12; 1 CHR 3.5; 2 SAM 24 · 1 CHR 21; 2 CHR 29–31; 2 CHR 33.10–20; KNOPPERS, 1 CHRONICLES"
# Subtraction and addition
The omissions are the famous part and the least interesting way to read the book. Chronicles also adds a great deal Samuel–Kings doesn't have, and keeps damaging material it could easily have dropped. Study the pattern and not only the gaps.
tabrl:
| Material | What Chronicles does with it |
| Bathsheba and Uriah | The story isn't retold. Bathsheba isn't erased. She appears in David's genealogy as the mother of Solomon and three other sons, under the name Bath-shua (1 Chr 3.5). The dynastic record keeps her, and the scandal is absent. |
| Amnon, Tamar, Absalom | The household violence and the revolt aren't retold. The reign comes through war, worship and preparation for the temple instead. |
| David's census | Kept, with the plague and the deaths. Where Samuel says the LORD incited David, Chronicles says an adversary did (1 Chr 21.1). The sin stays and the attribution moves. INTERPRETATION: A book doing pure whitewash wouldn't have kept the chapter. |
| Levites, singers, musicians | Added, extensively. Named guilds, rotas, instruments and duties Samuel–Kings barely mentions. For what a working sanctuary staff looked like, this is the source. |
| Hezekiah's Passover | Added. A mass reform and festival, with invitations sent north to people outside Judah's jurisdiction. A reunification liturgy (2 Chr 30). |
| Manasseh | Added. Kings gives Judah's worst king no redemption. Chronicles has him taken to Babylon with hooks, pray and be restored (2 Chr 33.11–13). INTERPRETATION: The most theologically loaded addition in the book, since it says no reign is beyond repentance. |
box: How to read the omissions honestly
A retelling that assumes its reader knows the older account isn't the same as one that hides it, and Chronicles points readers to other records. CONTESTED: "Cover-up" is one available reading. Another is a history written for worship more than for judgment. Hold both, and say which one you're using.
/end
prompt: The omission I find hardest to read charitably, and why | fill

